<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 1275–<a href="/years/1313">1313</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/13th-century">13th century</a> · Also known as Henry of Luxembourg · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-luxembourg">House of Luxembourg</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Henry VII was count of Luxembourg, elected king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in 1308 and crowned emperor in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> in 1312, the first since Frederick II. On his Italian expedition of 1310–1313 he tried to restore imperial authority, naming imperial vicars such as the Pico at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mirandola">Mirandola</a> and besieging rebellious <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brescia">Brescia</a> in 1311. He died near Siena in 1313. His son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-of-bohemia">John the Blind</a> became king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bohemia">Bohemia</a>, founding Luxembourg power there.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/henry-vii-holy-roman-emperor.jpg" alt="Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor" width="320" height="560" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Sailko · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Tino_di_camaino,_elementi_dal_monumento_funebre_di_arrigo_VII,_1315,_09_l%27imperatore_in_trono_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Angevin Naples spent much of the fourteenth century trying to recover Sicily through repeated expeditions that failed, and its kings led the Guelf cause in Italy: Robert the Wise was lord of Genoa from 1318 and the chief opponent of the emperor <b>Henry VII</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-naples">Kingdom of Naples · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Brescia joined the Lombard League against Frederick Barbarossa, resisted a long siege by Frederick II in 1238 and another by <b>Henry VII</b> in 1311.</blockquote><a href="/state/commune-of-brescia">Commune of Brescia</a></li><li><blockquote>Count <b>Henry VII</b> became emperor in 1308, and his son John the Blind, king of Bohemia, died at Crécy in 1346.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-luxembourg">County of Luxembourg</a></li><li><blockquote>Eberhard I fought the Swabian imperial cities and <b>Emperor Henry VII</b>, and Stuttgart became the residence in the 14th century.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-wurttemberg">County of Württemberg</a></li><li><blockquote>The Pico family held lands around Mirandola, north of Modena, and in 1311 Francesco Pico was named imperial vicar there by <b>Henry VII</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/duchy-of-mirandola">Duchy of Mirandola</a></li><li><blockquote>Archbishop Baldwin of Luxembourg, brother of <b>Emperor Henry VII</b>, consolidated the territory between 1307 and 1354.</blockquote><a href="/state/electorate-of-trier">Electorate of Trier</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valenciennes">Valenciennes</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Buonconvento, in what is now <a href="/countries/italy">Italy</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=50.36,3.52&amp;year=1299" aria-label="Valenciennes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4630 414 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Henry VII, Holy Roman Emperor"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5080 684h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5080 684h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=50.36,3.52&amp;year=1299">What was here in 1299? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
